From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:36:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Strict selector lists: reject trailing input after a valid
 prefix

querySelector("::before *") parsed as "::before" and matched:
parse_selector breaks out of its compound loop at AFTER_PSEUDO with
input remaining, and parse_until_before silently discards the rest
of the chunk up to the comma. Forgiving mode already treats a
non-exhausted chunk as an invalid selector; strict mode returned the
truncated prefix as if the author had written it. Require exhaustion
on the strict path so DOM query APIs throw SyntaxError and style
rules drop, matching Gecko/Blink. WPT css-pseudo parsing files pin
both the throw and the CSS.supports(selector()) answer.
---
 parser.rs | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/parser.rs b/parser.rs
index 3f835a9..fe2ecba 100644
--- a/parser.rs
+++ b/parser.rs
@@ -571,6 +571,12 @@ impl<Impl: SelectorImpl> SelectorList<Impl> {
                 if forgiving && (selector.is_err() || input.expect_exhausted().is_err()) {
                     input.expect_no_error_token()?;
                     selector = Ok(Selector::new_invalid(input.slice_from(start)));
+                } else if selector.is_ok() {
+                    // Strict mode must reject trailing input after a valid
+                    // prefix ("::before *" stops at AFTER_PSEUDO):
+                    // parse_until_before would otherwise silently discard
+                    // the rest of the chunk up to the comma.
+                    input.expect_exhausted()?;
                 }
                 selector
             })?;
